{{also|Lingo|lìn-gò͘}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|lingua||language}} + {{affix|en|-o|pos1=diminutive suffix}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlɪŋ.ɡəʊ/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈliŋ.ɡoʊ/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-lingo.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋɡəʊ|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|s|lingoes}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} Language, especially [[language]] peculiar to a particular group, field, or region; [[jargon]] or a [[dialect]].
#* {{RQ:Congreve Way of the World|scene=xv|page=47|passage=[...] I have Thoughts to tarry a ſmall Matter in Town, to learn ſomewhat of your '''''Lingo''''' firſt, before I croſs the Seas.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1846|author=George W.M. Reynolds|title=The Mysteries of London|volume=1|location=London|publisher=George Vickers|page=327
|passage="You see, ma'am, I can't divest myself of my professional '''lingo'''," observed Mr. Banks.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=12|passage=Nobody could make out plain what she said, for she was mainly jabbering Swede '''lingo''', but there was English enough, of a kind, to give us some idee.}}
# {{lb|en|Australian Aboriginal}} An [[Aboriginal]] language.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author={{w|Melissa Lucashenko}}|title=Too Much Lip|publisher=University of Queensland Press|year_published=2023|page=105
|passage=Granny Ava was the link: the last heathen of the family to speak the '''lingo''' fluently, before the Church waltzed in and jammed the Lord's Prayer in Granny Ruth's twelve-year-old mouth instead.}}

